tched model weights or an API. But the task says \"repositories and compute are not required\" — designs only. So I just design it.\n\nLet me now think about the concrete design sketch (high-level, for plan stage):\n\n**Design sketch:**\n- Experimental units: 23 BBH tasks (few-shot version, the de-facto standard). Optionally subset 5-8 tasks (e.g., the ones where OPRO reported gains: Word Sorting, Multistep Arithmetic Two, Penguins in a Table, plus diverse others) as a primary analysis, with all 23 as a robustness check if budget allows.\n- Conditions: (A) OPRO-optimized instruction per task, (B) \"Let's think step by step.\", (C) empty prompt. Optionally (D) human-written task-specific instructions (from BBH CoT author solutions) as an additional reference point — but the question only asks A vs B vs C.\n- Pipeline:\n  1. Split each task's data: optimization/training subset (e.g., 50-200 examples per task) and held-out test set (the rest of BBH's ~3-1000 examples; BBH tasks have varying sizes — actually BBH few-shot dev set has 1000 examples for many tasks... hmm, need to verify. BBH dev set: each task has a certain number of test examples; the few-shot subset provides 3-shot exemplars. Actually BBH provides 250 few-shot? Let me recall: BBH dev split is generated with the full test set for scoring (task.json with examples) and few-shot exemplars. Number of instances varies; many tasks have ~250 examples? I'm not certain. This is an uncertainty to confirm.)\n  2. OPRO loop: for each task (or shared across tasks?), maintain meta-prompt of candidate instructions with scores; each iteration, optimizer LLM generates B new candidates; evaluate each on the training subset via solver LLM; score = accuracy; keep top-k; repeat for T iterations. \n  3. Final instruction = best-scoring candidate on training subset; evaluate on held-out test subset with solver LLM.\n  4. Baselines: same solver, same test subset, same exemplars, with B and C prompts.\n- Replications: R optimizer seeds (e.g., 5) → distribution of OPRO outcomes; each seed yields a full optimization run. Solver sampling: temperature 0 for comparability, or multiple samples with self-consistency as a variant.\n- Metrics: primary per-task and mean accuracy; exact-match normalized scoring (strip punctuation, lowercase) following BBH evaluation; paired tests.\n- Analysis: paired per-task deltas (OPRO − baseline) with 95% bootstrap CIs; Wilcoxon signed-rank over 23 tasks; McNemar's test per task; report win/tie/loss counts; apply FDR correction for multiple comparisons. Also report effect size and practical significance (e.g., how many tasks improve by >1pt).\n- Controls: identical exemplar ordering, decoding params, prompt templates (same structure, only instruction string changes), fixed seeds; exclude test examples from any optimizer exposure (no leakage); separate the optimizer's \"learning\" data from evaluation data strictly.\n- Feasibility: cost estimate — number of LLM calls = T steps × B candidates × (training_examples per eval) + test calls. With T=10, B=4, train=50, tasks=23, that's 10×4×50×23 = 46,000 solver calls for optimization, plus test calls 23×test_examples×3 conditions. That's significant; with a small open model on CPU 224 cores it's feasible but slow; with API it costs money. Provide tiered budget options.\n- Sensitivity/robustness: vary evaluation subset size; check instruction transfer between test/validation; compare OPRO vs \"greedy human baseline\" instructions; optional self-consistency (k=3) to reduce solver variance.\n\nNow, the deliverables for plan stage: inventory, sketch, assumptions/uncertainties. Let me write these concisely but completely.\n\nActually, note the instructions: end_researcher_turn with stage=plan proposes approach, consequential choices and uncertainties, then wait for explicit approval before developing full design. The user (researcher) explicitly asked for the three items and to discuss before authorizing. So I should produce t